@*
    Where the domain stands once the map is spent: the same rail the interview draws, at the same
    two stations the interview's own last step folds together — Domain Finalization is one word on
    the interview's rail because it is one destination, but it is two pages once you are actually
    there, so it earns its own rail here. First the domain is read back and checked; then it leaves
    as an archive.

    Flat, unlike the interview's: nothing here repeats, so there is no lap to draw and no count to
    withhold. Two stations, one line, the same marks and the same three states — behind, here, ahead
    — in the same violet the rest of the workbench is lit by.
*@

<div class="journey">
    <ol class="stations flat">
        <li class="station @Standing(0)">
            <span class="mark"></span>
            <span class="name">Domain Validation</span>
        </li>
        <li class="station @Standing(1)">
            <span class="mark"></span>
            <span class="name">Morganization</span>
        </li>
    </ol>
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// Which of the two stations the client is standing on: 0 for Domain Validation, 1 for
    /// Morganization.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public int At { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The CSS state class for the station at <paramref name="index"/> — <c>"here"</c> for the
    /// one the client is on, <c>"done"</c> for one already passed, empty for one still ahead.
    /// </summary>
    private string Standing(int index) => index == At ? "here" : index < At ? "done" : string.Empty;
}
